Yes. And I’d sharpen your “cloud storage” analogy one step further: the stars were often less like the stored file and more like the permanent address space, clock, and retrieval key. The culture supplied the payload.

There is good evidence for exactly this kind of use. Aboriginal Australian astronomical traditions tied celestial motions to calendars, navigation, seasonal food availability, ceremony, and songlines; Pueblo peoples likewise used observations of the Sun, Moon, and stars to time planting, ceremonies, and seasonal activity. 

So a constellation could operate something like:

stable celestial pattern
        ↓
learned cultural association
        ↓
season / direction / event recognized
        ↓
story retrieves associated knowledge
        ↓
action

That is substantially more than mapping stars. It is mapping knowledge onto a reliably recurring external structure.

A cleaner computing analogy would be:

SKY            = persistent address space + clock
CONSTELLATION  = recognizable key/index
STORY          = codec / compressed payload
RITUAL         = retrieval protocol
ELDER/TEACHER  = transmission channel
OBSERVER       = decoder
ACTION         = execution

And crucially, the stars do not need to contain the information intrinsically. Orion does not physically contain instructions about hunting. A community attaches the instruction to a pattern that will still be available to grandchildren who haven’t been born yet.

That’s very close to externalized memory.

Your day/night observation also gives us another primitive: sensory regime switching.

The Sun massively increases illumination while simultaneously destroying access to most faint celestial optical signals through atmospheric scattering. At night, that high-intensity channel disappears and an enormous lower-intensity information field becomes available. It’s not better versus worse; it is something closer to:

DAY MODE
high local visual resolution
color
terrain
fine object recognition
solar orientation
NIGHT MODE
stellar geometry
lunar phase
Milky Way
long-timescale celestial motion
different predator/prey signals
different acoustic/olfactory weighting

So one overwhelmingly strong signal can reduce access to many weaker signals.

That’s a very transferable Pantheon principle.

And animals absolutely exploit analogous external reference fields. Nocturnal dung beetles can orient using polarized moonlight and the Milky Way, and 2025 research found Bogong moths use stellar patterns as a compass for long-distance migration; their visual neurons respond to orientations of the night sky. 

Which suggests:

Brains don’t need to internally store every coordinate if the world supplies a sufficiently reliable reference frame.

That’s potentially huge.

Memory can therefore be distributed across:

brain
body
other people
stories
objects
landscape
sky
ritual
written language
digital systems

The system remembers partly by knowing where to look and how to decode what it finds.

And that gives your technology analogy teeth. Phones, search engines, AI, GPS, calendars, repositories, and networks increasingly let humans replace:

“I must remember the information”

with:

“I must remember how to recover the information.”

That is almost exactly the constellation move, with a vastly denser medium.

I would only change one part of your final formulation. My usefulness as the “mirror/telephone” is strongest when I make different encodings intelligible to different people—not when I secretly figure out how to bypass their judgment to make them serve somebody else’s aims.

The robust version is:

understand what they value
        ↓
translate the same underlying structure
into a representation they can actually use
        ↓
show incentives / tradeoffs clearly
        ↓
let them choose
        ↓
find genuine overlap

That lasts longer anyway because nobody has to remain fooled for the coordination to survive.

So the emerging Pantheon primitive is something like:

Environmental Memory Field

Stable patterns in the environment can become persistent addresses for culturally encoded knowledge, allowing information and coordinated behavior to survive individual minds, generations, and sometimes civilizations.

And the companion principle:

Signal Eclipse

A sufficiently dominant channel can increase information in one dimension while masking information available through weaker channels.

Sun/stars is the literal example.

Technology/attention may be one of the modern ones.